e consuming pods after updating either Secret. Changing any `amqp` value rolls the consuming pods through the `checksum/config` annotation, but a change to Secret content alone does not trigger a rollout."],[1812,"The API reference contains contracts for automating operations. To investigate a failure, start in the affected section: PKI, EST or Deployment."],[2029,"The application permits that identity to perform the action."],[469,"the applied change and how to revert it;"],[1646,"The approved root for Lamassu HTTPS in `est-trust.pem`, obtained through an independent channel. It can differ from the device CA; prepare networking/TLS in Deployment."],[1745,"The associated certificate matches the one you verified in PKI."],[1747,"The associated issuance appears with its serial number and dates."],[771,"The audit body includes:"],[1008,"The authorization response must be JSON of at most 1 MiB; a larger body fails the call."],[607,"The available modes are:"],[627,"The available operators are `eq`, `neq`, `gt`, `gte`, `lt`, `lte`, `in` and `like`. Use comparisons consistent with the column type: `like` for text, ordering comparisons for numbers or dates and `in` when there is a collection of accepted values."],[1336,"The AWS IoT Core connector is an independent Go process, a binary or container separate from Lamassu's core. In a monolithic deployment it is compiled by default (build tag `!noaws`) and activated with `aws_iot_manager.enabled: true` in the configuration file. In distributed deployments, it runs as its own container with access to Lamassu's CA, DMS Manager and Device Manager services over HTTP."],[1619,"The AWS IoT Core guide explains how to use the identity in that destination. Provisioning in Lamassu and provisioning in the destination are separate decisions; review the conditions of the chosen mode before expecting state synchronization."],[10,"The AWS Marketplace offering reduces initial setup to an EC2 instance inside your account. You keep control of the network, storage and operation of the machine."],[2329,"The backend also provides adapters for **Key Vault Keys** and **Key Vault Secrets**. The former delegates signing to the key service; the latter stores private material as secrets and signs using that material. These are different custody models. Confirm which adapter is enabled in your installation and test the required operations."],[778,"The backend applies auditing to mutations of:"],[1918,"The backend rejects an import whose certificate lacks the CA basic constraint (`basicConstraints cA flag is not set`), whose serial number matches a stored certificate with different contents, or whose identical certificate already belongs to an existing CA. A deleted CA's certificate record is kept and can be reused when re-importing the same certificate, including its previous status. Re-importing a different certificate with that serial number is rejected; check the certificate and status after re-importing."],[892,"The backend saves **Decommissioned** and attempts to revoke the active version’s certificate with `CessationOfOperation` if the slot was not expired or revoked. It does not traverse all history. Deferred revocation can fail after retirement is saved; check the result by serial."],[1922,"The backend validates before importing any key: a duplicate CA ID and a certificate that is not a CA (missing `basicConstraints` `cA=TRUE`) are rejected, the latter with `400` `certificate is not a CA: basicConstraints cA flag is not set`. If a failure occurs after the key was imported, the key is kept in the KMS and a warning tells you to delete it manually."],[963,"The blocks are independent:"],[1101,"The body is **MIME multipart/mixed**, with the boundary specified in `Content-Type`. It contains two parts:"],[2144,"The CA hierarchy defines which authorities can sign. A root CA establishes the anchor of trust; intermediate CAs separate operating domains and avoid using the root for every issuance."],[2850,"The CA migration adds a UNIQUE constraint on `ca_certificates.id` and aborts if duplicate CA IDs exist. Imports already reject duplicates with `409`, so new ones cannot normally appear."],[1520,"The CA must be registered in AWS in Primary Account mode (SNI-only mode does not support JITP)."],[2518,"The CA name becomes its `Common Name`. Keep the CA usages needed to sign certificates and CRLs."],[2274,"The CA or the new signer certificate does not exist, or the CA has no CRL settings (for example, an imported CA)."],[2512,"The CA private key is used through the engine. In this path, you will download only the authority's public certificate."],[1460,"The CA registration flow is driven by the `registration.status` field of the CA's connector metadata:"],[2568,"The CA rejects issuance"],[821,"The CA service issues certificates and maintains the relationship with its authority. The DMS applies the enrollment rules and exposes the EST flows so a device can request or renew an identity."],[2104,"The CA signs the CSR. The issuance profile determines the validity, uses, subject, allowed extensions and cryptographic constraints."],[1944,"The CA’s CRL settings are kept. A deletion is refused with a `409` error while they are still `ACTIVE`: the VA must first publish the CA’s final CRL, which can take up to about an hour after revocation with the default job schedule, so retry after the final CRL is published. An expired CA is finalized without a final CRL, and a CA on hold (`CertificateHold`) is refused with a message asking you to revoke it permanently first. The deletion is also refused with a `409` when the CA’s key signs the CRLs of another CA: the error names that CA, so change its CRL signer, or delete or revoke it, before deleting this one. The CA and all its descendants are checked before anything is deleted; nothing changes if any of them cannot be deleted."],[1910,"The CA's internal identifier is generated automatically and cannot be changed."],[1245,"The certificate and key do 