hat depend on the PKI."],[622,"The data model shows how the resources used in entity rules are connected."],[561,"The decision is made **before forwarding the request to the service**:"],[583,"The decision is made with the domain operation context:"],[1055,"The default content is Base64-encoded DER PKCS#7. It is not a direct PEM file."],[1093,"The default response has `Content-Type: application/pkcs7-mime; smime-type=certs-only` and `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64`. The body is Base64-encoded DER PKCS#7 with lines of up to 76 characters. It includes the certificates selected by the DMS, not necessarily every ancestor."],[1269,"The default response is Base64-encoded DER PKCS#7; PEM through `Accept: application/x-pem-file` is a Lamassu extension. ServerKeyGen returns multipart and requires handling each part separately. See the EST reference."],[1096,"The default response uses the PKCS#7/Base64 format described for cacerts, but contains the issued certificate; do not assume it includes its entire chain. `Accept: application/x-pem-file` returns that certificate in PEM. The client retains the key that signed the CSR."],[327,"The default transport of these clients bounds the dial timeout (30 seconds), the TLS handshake (10 seconds) and idle connection reuse (90 seconds) so an unreachable dependency fails fast instead of hanging; these values are Go transport defaults, not Lamassu settings. HTTP/2 is negotiated when the peer supports it. The authorization-engine connector keeps its own transport without these dial and handshake timeouts; only its overall 30-second request timeout bounds each call."],[1791,"The device client requests a certificate and installs it with the matching key."],[836,"The device generates a key and requests a certificate through EST, or an operator assigns it an identity."],[2130,"The device history relates its previous and current identities. For administrative changes and mutation errors, see Audit logs."],[2151,"The device installs the certificate and the chain it needs."],[1522,"The device must present both its certificate and the issuing CA's certificate in the TLS handshake with AWS IoT Core."],[2148,"The device proves it can enroll according to the DMS policy."],[2113,"The device view may add operational states such as missing identity, pending renewal, expiring soon or decommissioned. These are an interpretation of the device's status and its certificates, not new X.509 states."],[1603,"The device's association with its certificates: an active version and a history."],[2146,"The DMS acts as the enrollment point. It can authenticate an EST request with a client certificate, an external webhook or a combination of both. CAs configured to validate the requester do not have to be the CA that will issue the new identity."],[1858,"The DMS delivers the correct chain through EST `/cacerts`."],[918,"The DMS first looks up the certificate in Lamassu; for external certificates it attempts OCSP and CRL. If revocation cannot be checked, validators can continue treating it as not revoked. The code also does not establish rejection of every external status other than Good. Test required behavior with your actual credential and endpoint; do not present this as guaranteed strict enforcement across every source."],[1294,"The DMS may include the available downstream TLS certificate, selected Managed CAs and enrollment CA. Selecting the issuer does not include every ancestor. The set may contain leaves, roots and intermediates, including multiple hierarchies during migration."],[912,"The DMS sends operation context to the configured service. Method comes from configuration; agree the contract before selecting POST or PUT. Authorization wait defaults to 10 seconds, replaced by a configured positive timeout. The DMS verifies the receiver certificate unless `insecure_skip_verify` is enabled in the webhook config — verification is on by default — and does not follow redirects, so the configured URL must be the final one.